 2^4=16\u22611, 2^5=2, etc period 4.\n\nSet equality:\n\nCase m mod5 =0 => LHS 0, RHS 2^m mod5 depends on m mod4. Need 0 \u22612^m mod5. But 2^m never 0 mod5. So impossible. Thus m not divisible by5.\n\nm mod5 =1 => LHS 1, need 2^m \u22611 mod5 => m \u22610 (mod4). So m \u22611 (mod5) and m \u22610 (mod4). Solve via CRT.\n\nm \u22611 (mod5), m \u22610 (mod4). Find smallest solution: numbers multiples of4: 0,4,8,12,16,20,... Check mod5: 0\u21920 no,4\u21924 no,8\u21923 no,12\u21922 no,16\u21921 yes. So m \u226116 (mod20). So m = 16+20k.\n\nm mod5 =2 => LHS 3, need 2^m \u22613 mod5. Compute 2^m mod5 values: for m mod4 =1\u21922,2\u21924,3\u21923,0\u21921. So need 3 => m \u22613 (mod4). So m \u22612 (mod5) and m \u22613 (mod4). Solve: numbers \u22612 mod5: 2,7,12,17,22,27,32,... Check mod4: 2\u21922 not 3,7\u21923 works. So m \u22617 (mod20). So m = 7+20k.\n\nm mod5 =3 => LHS 2, need 2^m \u22612 mod5 => m \u22611 (mod4). So m \u22613 (mod5) and m \u22611 (mod4). Numbers \u22613 mod5: 3,8,13,18,23,28,... Check mod4: 3\u21923 not1,8\u21920,13\u21921 works. So m \u226113 (mod20). So m =13+20k.\n\nm mod5 =4 => LHS 4, need 2^m \u22614 mod5 => m \u22612 (mod4). So m \u22614 (mod5) and m \u22612 (mod4). Numbers \u22614 mod5: 4,9,14,19,24,29,... Check mod4: 4\u21920 not2,9\u21921,14\u21922 works. So m \u226114 (mod20). So m =14+20k.\n\nThus all solutions for m not multiple of5 satisfy one of these four arithmetic progressions modulo20: m \u22617,13,14,16 (mod20). Good.\n\nNow we also have condition from earlier about n mod6 being 0 or 3.\n\nNow we need to find actual integer solutions where equation holds.\n\nLet's test small m values from the allowed residues.\n\nStart with m=1: check if any n works? Equation: 1 +5^n =7. So 5^n =6 not power of5. No.\n\nm=2: 8+5^n =49 =>5^n=41 not power.\n\nm=3:27+5^n=343 =>5^n=316 not power.\n\nm=4:64+5^n=2401 =>5^n=2337 not power.\n\nm=5 not allowed (multiple of5). m=6:216+5^n=117,649 =>5^n=961,603? Wait compute 7^6 =117,649. Subtract 216 =>