likely to be exact power of5. But maybe it works.\n\nWe can test using modulo higher powers of5, e.g., modulo 125.\n\nSince n\u22652, we need 5^n \u22610 mod125? Actually 5^2=25, 5^3=125. For n\u22653, divisible by125. So we can test modulo125.\n\nCompute 7^m mod125 and m^3 mod125, see if difference is 0 mod125.\n\nCompute 7^m mod125. Since \u03c6(125)=100, 7^100 \u22611 mod125. Need m mod100.\n\n74 mod100 =74. Compute 7^74 mod125 maybe using repeated squaring.\n\nAlternatively compute using known powers: 7^2=49; 7^4=49^2=2401 mod125: 125*19=2375 remainder 26. So 7^4 \u226126.\n\n7^8 =26^2=676 mod125: 125*5=625 remainder 51.\n\n7^16 =51^2=2601 mod125: 125*20=2500 remainder101.\n\n7^32 =101^2=10201 mod125: 125*81=10125 remainder76.\n\n7^64 =76^2=5776 mod125: 125*46=5750 remainder26. Interesting 7^64 \u226126 (same as 7^4). Good.\n\nNow we need 7^74 = 7^(64+4+4)?? Actually 64+4+4 =72, need extra 2. Let's do 7^64 *7^4 *7^4 *7^2? Wait 64+4+4+2 =74. So multiply accordingly.\n\nWe have 7^64 \u226126.\n7^4 \u226126.\n7^2 \u226149.\n\nThus product: 26 *26 *26 *49 mod125.\n\nFirst 26*26=676 \u226151 (as earlier). Then 51*26=1326 \u22611326-1250=96. Then 96*49=4704. 125*37=4625 remainder79. So 7^74 \u226179 mod125.\n\nNow compute m^3 mod125: 74^3. Compute 74^2=5476. Mod125: 125*43=5375 remainder101. Multiply by 74: 101*74=7474. 125*59=7375 remainder99. So m^3 \u226199 mod125.\n\nDifference: 79 - 99 = -20 \u2261105 mod125. So 7^m - m^3 \u2261105 mod125.\n\nWe need this to be divisible by 125 (if n\u22653) or at least 25 (if n=2). Since result \u2261105 mod125, not 0 mod125. For n=2, we need \u22610 mod25. 105 mod25 = 5. Not zero. So fails.\n\nThus m=74 not solution.\n\nNow continue checking other bases.\n\nBase 27: m mod4 =3 \u2192 7^m \u226118. Need m^3 \u226118 mod25. Residues for base 27 sequence: m \u226127,37,47,... add 10 each time. Compute residues mod25: 27\u21922, 37\u219212, 47\u219222, 57\u21927, 67\u219217, 77\u219227\u21922 again cycle length5? Actually adding 10 cycles through 5 distinct residues before repeating after 5 steps (since gcd(10,25)=5). So residues: 2,12,22,7,17.\n\nCompute cubes mod25:\n\n2^3=8\n12^3: 12^2=144\u2261 -1 (since 150-6) => -1? Actually 144-125=19. Multiply by12: 19*12=228 \u2261228-200=28 \u22613.\n22^3: 22\u2261 -3. (-3)^3 = -27 \u22613 (since -27+25=-2 \u226123). Wait compute directly: 22^2=484 mod25: 25*19=475 remainder9. 9*22=198 mod25: 25*7=175 remainder23. So 22^3 \u226123.\n7^3=343 mod25: 25*13=325 remainder18.\n17^3: 17^2=289 mod25: 25*11=275 remainder14. 14*17=238 mod25: 25*9=225 remainder13.\n\nThus residues: 8,3,23,18,13.\n\nWe need 18 (the 7^m value). Indeed 18 appears when m\u22617 mod25 (which corresponds to t=3). So m = 27 + 60*3 = 213? Wait base 27 + 60*t, t=3 gives 27+180=207. Check mod25: 207 mod25 = 207-200=7 correct. So m=207 satisfies modulo25 condition.\n\nNow test modulo125.\n\nCompute 7^207 mod125. Since period 100, reduce exponent mod100: 207 mod100 =7. So 7^207 \u22617^7 mod125.\n\nCompute 7^7: 7^4=26, 7^5=26*7=182 mod125=57, 7^6=57*7=399 mod125=24, 7^7=24*7=168 mod125=43.\n\nThus 7^207 \u226143.\n\nNow compute m^3 mod125: 207^3. Compute 207 mod125 =82. 82^2=6724. 125*53=6625 remainder99. Multiply by82: 99*82=8118. 125*64=8000 remainder118. So m^3 \u2261118.\n\nDifference: 43-118 = -75 \u226150 mod125. Not 0 mod125 nor 0 mod25 (50 mod25=0!). Actually 50 mod25 =0, so condition for n=2 (i.e., 5^2=25) would be satisfied (difference divisible by25). But need also that the quotient is a power of5 (specifically 5^n with n\u22652). The difference is 7^m - m^3 = 5^n. If n=2, then RHS =25. So we need 7^m - m^3 =25. Let's check if difference equals 25? We found modulo125 it's 50, not 25. So not.\n\nBut maybe n>2, requiring divisibility by125, which fails. So m=207 not solution.\n\nNext base 33: m mod4 =1 \u2192 7^m \u22617. Need m^3 \u22617 mod25. Sequence residues: start 33 mod25 =8, then +10 each step: 8,18,28\u21923,13,23,3, etc. Compute cubes:\n\n8^3=512 mod25=12 (as earlier)\n18^3: 18^2=324 mod25= -1? 324-300=24. 24*18=432 mod25=8.\n3^3=27 mod25=2.\n13^3: 13^2=169 mod25=19. 19*13=247 mod25=22.\n23^3: 23\u2261 -2. (-2)^3 = -8 \u226117.\n\nResidues: 12,8,2,22,17. Need 7, not present. So no solution for this base.\n\nBase 36: m mod4 =0 \u2192 7^m \u22611. Need m^3 \u22611 mod25. Sequence residues: start 36 mod25=11, then +10 each: 11,21,31\u21926,16,26\u21921, etc. Compute cubes:\n\n11^3=1331 mod25=6 (as earlier)\n21^3: 21\u2261 -4. (-4)^3 = -64 \u2261 -14 \u226111.\n6^3=216 mod25=16.\n16^3: 16^2=256 mod25=6. 6*16=96 mod25=21.\n1^3=1.\n\nResidues: 6,11,16,21,1. Need 1, which occurs when m\u22611 mod25 (i.e., after 4 steps). Starting from 36, after adding 60*4 =240, m=276. Check: 36+240=276, mod25 = 1 indeed. So m=276 satisfies modulo25 condition.\n\nNow test modulo125.\n\nCompute 7^276 mod125. Exponent mod100: 276 mod100 =76. So need 7^76. From earlier we had 7^64 \u226126, 7^8 \u226151. Multiply: 26*51=1326 \u226196 (as earlier). So 7^76 \u226196.\n\nCompute m^3 mod125: 276 mod125 =276-250=26. 26^2=676 mod125=51. 51*26=1326 mod125=96. So m^3 \u226196.\n\nDifference: 96-96=0 mod125. Great! So difference divisible by125. Good.\n\nNow we need to see if the quotient is a power of5.\n\nCompute Q = 7^m - m^3 = 5^n.\n\nWe know Q is divisible by125. Let's compute Q modulo 625 maybe to determine n.\n\nWe need to compute 7^276 - 276^3 mod 625.\n\nWe can compute using modular exponentiation.\n\nFirst compute 7^276 mod 625. Since \u03c6(625)=500, exponent reduction not simple. We'll compute via successive squares maybe.\n\nBut maybe we can argue that if Q is a power of5, then Q \u22610 mod5 but not divisible by any other primes. Since we have congruences, maybe we can deduce n.\n\nWe can compute Q/125 = (7^m - m^3)/125. Need to see if that quotient is a power of5 (i.e., 5^{n-2}). Could be 1,5,25,...\n\nLet's compute Q modulo 625 to see if divisible by 250 etc.\n\nCompute 7^276 mod 625.\n\nWe can compute using Python style mentally? Might be lengthy but doable with modular exponent.\n\nLet's compute powers of 7 mod 625.\n\nWe can compute 7^2=49\n7^4=49^2=2401 mod625: 625*3=1875 remainder526.\n7^8 =526^2 mod625. Compute 526^2 = (500+26)^2 =250000+2*500*26+676 =250000+26000+676=276,? Actually 250000+26000=276000, +676=276676. Divide by625: 625*442=276250 remainder426. So 7^8 \u2261426.\n\n7^16 =426^2 mod625. 426^2 = (400+26)^2 =160000+2*400*26+676=160000+20800+676=181476. 625*290=181250 remainder226. So 7^16 \u2261226.\n\n7^32 =226^2=51076. 625*81=50625 remainder451. So \u2261451.\n\n7^64 =451^2=203401. 625*325=203125 remainder276. So \u2261276.\n\nGreat! So 7^64 \u2261276.\n\nNow we need 7^276 = 7^(64*4 + 4) because 64*4=256, plus 20? Wait 256+20=276. So 7^276 = 7^256 * 7^20.\n\nWe have 7^64 \u2261276. Then 7^128 = (7^64)^2 \u2261276^2 mod625. Compute 276^2 = (200+76)^2 =40000+2*200*76+5776=40000+30400+5776=76176. 625*122=76250 remainder -74 => 625-74=551. So 7^128 \u2261551.\n\nThen 7^256 = (7^128)^2 \u2261551^2 mod625. Compute 551^2 = (500+51)^2 =250000+2*500*51+2601=250000+51000+2601=303601. 625*486=303750 remainder -149 => 625-149=476. So 7^256 \u2261476.\n\nNow compute 7^20. We can compute 7^10 then square.\n\n7^5 = 7^4*7 =526*7=3682 mod625: 625*5=3125 remainder557. So 7^5 \u2261557.\n\n7^10 =557^2 mod625. 557^2 = (500+57)^2 =250000+2*500*57+3249=250000+57000+3249=310249. 625*496=310000 remainder249. So 7^10 \u2261249.\n\n7^20 =249^2 mod625. 249^2 = (250-1)^2 =62500 -500 +1 =62001. 625*99=61875 remainder126. So 7^20 \u2261126.\n\nNow multiply: 7^276 = 7^256 * 7^20 \u2261476 *126 mod625.\n\nCompute 476*126 = 476*100 + 476*26 =47600 + 12376 =59976. Mod625: 625*96=599... 625*96=599... 625*96=599... compute 625*96=599... 625*100=62500, subtract 625*4=2500 => 62500-2500=599... actually 625*96 = 625*(100-4)=62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599... 62500-2500=599